Subject: Answer parseInt(<number>) From The Number

What this does
  js_parseInt answers a numeric argument with a decimal radix directly: an int32
  argument is returned unchanged, and a float64 in 1 <= |d| < 1e21 returns
  trunc(d).

Why we need it
  The general path converts the number to a decimal string, copies it out to a
  second buffer, parses the digits back and frees both -- two allocations, a dtoa
  and an atof to recompute a value the caller already had. Compiled JavaScript
  uses parseInt() as an integer cast on values that are already numbers.

What it adds
  Admission is the radix being absent, undefined, 0 or 10, and the argument being
  an int, or a float in the fixed-point window where Number::toString produces
  [-]digits[.digits]. Everything else -- NaN, the infinities, 1e21, 1e-7 and the
  -0-producing cases -- keeps the general path rather than being approximated. A
  number argument's ToString and an int or undefined radix's ToInt32 cannot call
  user code, so no observable step is skipped.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -51459,7 +51459,24 @@
     int flags;
     int32_t radix;
     JSValue ret;
+
+    if (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(argv[1]) == JS_TAG_UNDEFINED ||
+         (JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(argv[1]) == JS_TAG_INT &&
+          (JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[1]) == 0 ||
+           JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[1]) == 10))) {
+        int vtag = JS_VALUE_GET_TAG(argv[0]);
 
+        if (vtag == JS_TAG_INT)
+            return js_int32(JS_VALUE_GET_INT(argv[0]));
+        if (JS_TAG_IS_FLOAT64(vtag)) {
+            double d = JS_VALUE_GET_FLOAT64(argv[0]);
+            double ad = fabs(d);
+
+            if (ad >= 1.0 && ad < 1e21)
+                return js_number(trunc(d));
+        }
+    }
+
     str = JS_ToCString(ctx, argv[0]);
     if (!str)
         return JS_EXCEPTION;
